/* Final "Interview loop" week for the ds, ba and fnd programs. First option in every multiple choice is correct (shuffled at runtime). */
(function () {
  const { T, Q, F, O, RK, M, BN, BUG, NUM, L } = GR.ivKit;

  /* ================= DATA SCIENCE ================= */
  GR.courseAddUnit("ds", {
    title: "Interview loop", sub: "Stats, product sense, ML and the follow-ups",
    lessons: [
      L("The questions they actually ask", "ds.hypo", [
        T(`<p>A data science loop usually mixes a <b>stats round</b>, a <b>product or experiment round</b>, an <b>ML round</b> and a coding or SQL screen. The questions below are commonly asked themes. The pattern that wins: say the plain-language answer first, then add the nuance. Jargon-first answers sound like you memorized a flashcard.</p>`),
        Q(`"Explain a p-value to a product manager." Which answer is strongest?`,
          [`If there were truly no effect, how often we would see data at least this extreme; a small value means that data would be surprising.`, `The probability that the null hypothesis is true given the data we observed; a small value means the null is probably false.`, `The probability that our result is a fluke and will not replicate if we ran the same experiment again next week.`, `The size of the effect: the smaller the p-value, the bigger the win we should expect after launch.`],
          `A p-value is computed assuming the null is true. It says nothing directly about the chance the null is true, about replication, or about effect size.`),
        Q(`"What does a 95% confidence interval mean?"`,
          [`If we repeated the study many times, about 95% of intervals built this way would contain the true value.`, `There is a 95% probability that the true value lies inside this one particular interval we computed from our sample.`, `95% of the individual data points in the sample fall inside the interval we calculated.`, `We are 95% sure that the sample mean we measured is inside the interval built around it.`],
          `The 95% describes the long-run behavior of the method. Once computed, a specific interval either contains the truth or it does not. The sample mean is always in its own interval, and the interval is about the mean, not about individual points.`),
        Q(`Your model has 2% error on training data and 18% on validation data. Diagnose it and propose a fix.`,
          [`High variance (overfitting): get more data, regularize, simplify the model, or stop earlier.`, `High bias (underfitting): add more features and use a more complex model so it can capture more of the pattern.`, `Unlucky split: reshuffle the rows and re-split until validation error drops close to the training error.`, `The metric is wrong: switch to a metric where the gap between training and validation disappears.`],
          `A big train/validation gap means the model memorizes noise. Underfit models have high error on both sets. Reshuffling until the number looks better is just tuning on your validation data.`),
        Q(`20% of the income column is missing. What is your first question?`,
          [`Why is it missing: at random, or tied to the value itself (such as high earners skipping it)?`, `Can I drop every row with a missing value, since most of the data would still be left to model?`, `Can I fill them all with the column mean and move on, since that keeps the average unchanged?`, `Can I fill them with zero so the column stays numeric and the pipeline does not break?`],
          `The mechanism decides which fixes are safe. If missingness depends on income, dropping or mean-filling biases everything downstream.`),
        Q(`PM: "How long do we run the test?" Strongest answer?`,
          [`Fix baseline, minimum detectable effect, alpha and power first, compute the sample size, convert to days with traffic, and round up to full weeks.`, `Run until the p-value on the primary metric first drops below 0.05, then stop the test, call it a win, and ship it to everyone that same afternoon.`, `One day is enough because traffic is large, and a very big sample makes a single day as reliable as a longer test.`, `Run until the lift line on the dashboard looks stable for a day or two, then stop; the exact length does not matter much.`],
          `Sample size is decided before launch. Stopping when p crosses 0.05 inflates false positives, and whole weeks cover weekday effects.`),
        BN(`Which of these support a causal claim, and which are correlation only?`, [`Randomized experiment supports causation`, `Observational: correlation only`],
          [[`A random half of users saw the new checkout, and their conversion was higher`, 0], [`Users of the mobile app spend more than users who skip it`, 1], [`Cities with more ice cream sales have more drownings`, 1], [`Coupons were handed out by coin flip, then spend was compared`, 0], [`Customers who opened the email renewed more often`, 1]],
          `Random assignment breaks the link between who gets the treatment and everything else about them. In the observational cases, hidden factors (heat, engagement, intent) can drive both sides.`),
        Q(`Puzzle: a family has two children and at least one is a boy. What is the probability both are boys?`,
          [`1/3`, `1/2`, `1/4`, `2/3`],
          `The equally likely outcomes are BB, BG and GB (GG is ruled out). Only one of the three is BB.`),
        NUM(`A disease affects 1% of people. A test catches 99% of cases (sensitivity) and correctly clears 99% of healthy people (specificity). You test positive. What is the probability you have the disease, in percent?`, 50,
          `Out of 10,000 people: 100 sick, 99 test positive; 9,900 healthy, 99 test positive. So 99 of 198 positives are real: 50%.`, undefined, 0.01, `%`),
        Q(`"The correlation between x and y is 0.0. So x tells us nothing about y." Your reply?`,
          [`Not necessarily: correlation measures only linear association, so a curved relationship such as y = x squared can show near-zero correlation.`, `Correct: a correlation of exactly zero means the two variables are statistically independent of each other, so x is useless.`, `Correct, as long as the sample is above 30, because then the estimate is reliable enough to rule out any relationship between x and y.`, `Wrong: the correlation must be hidden by outliers every time, so removing them would reveal a clear linear relationship.`],
          `Zero correlation rules out a linear trend, not every relationship. Independence implies zero correlation, but not the reverse.`),
        F(`Fill in the standard error of a sample mean.`, `se = sd / ___`, [`sqrt(n)`], [`n`, `n**2`, `log(n)`],
          `The standard error shrinks with the square root of n, which is why quadrupling the sample only halves the uncertainty.`),
      ], "learn"),

      L("Say it out loud", "ds.causal", [
        T(`<p>Interviewers score <b>how you think</b> as much as the answer. Speak in three layers: a one-sentence headline, two or three supporting points, then the caveat and the next step. If you are stuck, say your assumption and work a tiny example aloud. Silence reads as panic, even when it is just thinking.</p>`),
        O(`The test shows +2% conversion with p = 0.03. "Do we ship?" Order a strong spoken answer.`,
          [`Restate the result and the decision it feeds`, `Check the setup: randomization, sample ratio, duration`, `Read the confidence interval for practical size, not just significance`, `Check guardrail metrics and key segments`, `Recommend ship or hold, and name the remaining risk`],
          `Validity first, then magnitude, then side effects, then a decision. A recommendation with a named risk beats "it depends."`),
        RK(`Rank these openings for "Explain overfitting," strongest first.`,
          [`Plain definition, a concrete example, then how you would detect and fix it`, `Plain definition, then a pile of jargon about VC dimension`, `Only the formula for the bias-variance decomposition`, `"It is when the model is bad."`],
          `Definition, example, and action show you can teach and apply the idea. Formula-only and jargon-only answers hide whether you understand it, and the last one says nothing.`),
        RK(`Rank these kinds of evidence for a causal claim from weakest to strongest.`,
          [`A single anecdote`, `A cross-sectional correlation`, `A regression with controls`, `A natural experiment or difference-in-differences`, `A randomized experiment`],
          `Each step removes more room for hidden confounders. Randomization removes them by design.`),
        M(`Match each question to the key point a strong answer must hit.`,
          [[`Why not just drop rows with missing values?`, `It can bias results if missingness relates to the outcome, and it shrinks the sample`], [`Why is accuracy misleading for fraud detection?`, `Fraud is rare, so predicting "never fraud" scores high while catching nothing`], [`Why not peek at an A/B test every day?`, `Repeated looks inflate the false positive rate unless you use sequential methods`], [`Why does regularization help?`, `It penalizes complexity, trading a little bias for lower variance`], [`Why keep a holdout set?`, `It estimates performance on data the model never touched`]],
          `A good answer is one claim and one reason. Each right-hand phrase is the "because" the interviewer is listening for.`),
        M(`Match each term to a plain-English phrase you could say to a non-statistician.`,
          [[`Type I error`, `Calling nothing a win (a false alarm)`], [`Type II error`, `Missing a real effect`], [`Power`, `Chance of detecting a real effect of the planned size`], [`Confidence interval`, `Range of plausible values for the true effect`], [`Standard error`, `How much the estimate would wobble across repeated samples`]],
          `If you can say it in one plain sentence, you understand it. If you can only say it in symbols, expect a follow-up.`),
        Q(`The interviewer asks "Why that metric?" Best answer?`,
          [`Tie it to the decision: it moves when the goal moves, is hard to game, and is measurable inside the test window.`, `It is the standard metric that most teams in our industry use, so the result will be easy to compare against others.`, `It was the easiest one to pull from the warehouse, and a metric we can compute quickly beats a perfect one.`, `It had the largest lift in our pilot, so it gives the experiment the best chance to show a clear win.`],
          `Metric choice is a product argument, not a convenience. Choosing the metric that showed the biggest lift is p-hacking in disguise.`),
        Q(`You blank on a stats question mid-interview. Best move?`,
          [`Say what you do know, state an assumption, and work a small example aloud.`, `Stay quiet and think until the answer comes back, since speaking will only make you lose the thread.`, `Guess a formula with confidence, because sounding sure matters more than being right in a live stats round.`, `Say you have not studied that topic and ask to move on to a different question immediately.`],
          `Interviewers hand out hints to candidates they can follow. A tiny worked example often reveals the answer and always shows your process.`),
      ], "learn"),

      L("Follow-ups they throw at you", "ds.hypo", [
        T(`<p>The first answer is the warm-up. The <b>follow-up</b> is where the grade is decided: "okay, now what if the prevalence is lower?" or "what if the effect is half as big?" Practice changing one number and re-deriving, out loud.</p>`),
        Q(`You said p = 0.03. Follow-up: "So there is a 97% chance the feature works?"`,
          [`No. The p-value assumes there is no effect; it is not the probability the feature works.`, `Yes, one minus the p-value is the probability that the feature genuinely works, so 97% is the number to quote.`, `Yes, but only if the sample is large enough, since a large sample makes the p-value read as a probability of success.`, `No, it means the feature works for 3% of users and fails for the other 97% of them.`],
          `P(data this extreme | no effect) is not P(effect | data). Getting from the second requires a prior, which is a Bayesian question.`),
        NUM(`Mean 50, standard deviation 10, n = 100. What is the half-width of the 95% confidence interval for the mean (use 1.96)?`, 1.96,
          `SE = 10 / sqrt(100) = 1. The half-width is 1.96 x 1 = 1.96.`, undefined, 0.01),
        NUM(`Follow-up: you want that half-width cut in half. What sample size do you need?`, 400,
          `Half-width scales with 1 / sqrt(n). To halve it, multiply n by 4: 100 x 4 = 400.`),
        NUM(`You run 20 independent tests, each at alpha = 0.05, and no real effects exist. What is the chance of at least one false positive, in percent?`, 64.15,
          `P(no false positive) = 0.95^20, about 0.358. So P(at least one) = 1 - 0.358, about 0.642, or about 64%.`, undefined, 0.5, `%`),
        NUM(`Follow-up: with a Bonferroni correction for those 20 tests, what per-test threshold keeps the family-wise error at 0.05?`, 0.0025,
          `Divide alpha by the number of tests: 0.05 / 20 = 0.0025.`, undefined, 0.00001),
        Q(`The overall A/B result is flat. Follow-up: "How would you look at segments responsibly?"`,
          [`Name a few segments in advance, correct for multiple comparisons, and treat any hit as a hypothesis for a follow-up test.`, `Slice by every available attribute and ship to whichever segment shows a statistically significant lift at the 0.05 level.`, `Skip segments entirely, because a flat overall result means no group of users can possibly have responded differently.`, `Drop the segments that look negative and re-run the analysis on the rest until the overall result turns significant.`],
          `Many slices guarantee some false positives. Pre-named segments with a correction, plus a confirmation test, keep the finding honest.`),
        Q(`You planned a 50/50 split but got 10,000 users in control and 9,600 in treatment. Follow-up: "Fine to analyze?"`,
          [`Suspect a sample ratio mismatch and debug assignment and logging before trusting any metric.`, `Fine: a 4% difference in group sizes is small, so analyze the metrics as planned and note the imbalance in a footnote.`, `Delete 400 control users at random so the groups match exactly, then analyze the rest as if the split had been even.`, `Fine, because having more control users makes the baseline estimate more stable and the comparison stronger.`],
          `With 19,600 users the expected count per arm is 9,800 with a standard deviation near 70. A gap of 200 is about 2.9 standard deviations, very unlikely by chance, so something broke upstream.`),
        NUM(`Control converted 200 of 10,000 users; treatment converted 230 of 10,000. What is the relative lift, in percent?`, 15,
          `Control is 2.0% and treatment is 2.3%. The absolute lift is 0.3 points; relative is 0.3 / 2.0 = 15%.`, undefined, 0.01, `%`),
        Q(`Follow-up: the true effect turns out to be half of what you planned for. How does the required sample size change?`,
          [`About four times larger`, `About twice as large`, `About half as large`, `Unchanged`],
          `Sample size scales with 1 / effect squared. Half the effect means four times the data.`),
        NUM(`Same disease test (99% sensitivity, 99% specificity) but the disease affects only 0.1% of people. You test positive. What is the probability of disease, in percent?`, 9,
          `Per 100,000 people: 100 sick, 99 positive; 99,900 healthy, 999 false positives. 99 / (99 + 999) is about 9%. Rare conditions make false positives dominate.`, undefined, 0.2, `%`),
        BUG(`This should return a two-sided p-value for a difference in means. Tap the buggy line.`,
          [`diff = treat.mean() - control.mean()`, `se = np.sqrt(treat.var() / len(treat) + control.var() / len(control))`, `z = diff / se`, `p = 1 - norm.cdf(z)`],
          3,
          `That is a one-sided p-value, and it gives a value near 1 when the effect is negative. A two-sided version is 2 * (1 - norm.cdf(abs(z))).`),
        Q(`Follow-up: "Would more training data fix this model's problem?" The model is underfitting, with high error on both train and validation sets.`,
          [`Not much: more data mainly cuts variance, but this is a bias problem, so use a richer model or better features.`, `Yes, more data always lowers both bias and variance, so collecting a larger training set is the first thing to try.`, `Yes, but only if you also remove most of the features, so the model has fewer inputs to fit to the extra rows.`, `No, nothing can fix underfitting, so the only option is to accept the error and choose a different problem.`],
          `A model too simple to capture the pattern stays too simple with more rows. Adding capacity or better features addresses bias.`),
        Q(`Follow-up: "What is wrong with filling missing values with the column mean?"`,
          [`It shrinks the variance and weakens correlations, making estimates look more certain than they are.`, `Nothing is wrong with it; the mean is always the safest choice because it leaves the column's average unchanged.`, `It cannot be used on numeric columns at all, because the mean of a column with gaps is undefined in pandas.`, `It raises the median of the column, and that shift in the center is the main way the fill damages the data.`],
          `Every filled value sits exactly at the center, so spread shrinks and relationships with other columns blur.`),
      ], "drill"),

      L("Mock loop", "ds.causal", [
        T(`<p>Full round, no hints. Twelve mixed questions in the order a real loop tends to throw them: stats, experiment, ML, then coding. Answer each one the way you would out loud, in one headline plus one reason.</p>`),
        Q(`A teammate says "the result is significant, so the effect is large." Your reply?`,
          [`Significance and size are different: with enough data tiny effects are significant, so check the confidence interval.`, `Agreed: a small p-value means the observed effect is large, since small effects would not be able to produce one.`, `Agreed, as long as p is below 0.01, because a stricter threshold guarantees the effect is also practically large.`, `Wrong: significant results are always tiny, because large effects tend to be flagged as outliers and discarded.`],
          `P-values depend on both effect and sample size. The interval shows how big the effect plausibly is.`),
        NUM(`You track a mean metric with standard deviation 10 and want to detect a change of 1. Using the rule of thumb n = 16 x sigma squared / delta squared, how many users per group?`, 1600,
          `16 x 100 / 1 = 1,600 per group. The rule assumes 80% power and a 5% two-sided alpha.`),
        O(`Order the steps of a sound experiment.`,
          [`Define the decision and the primary metric`, `Compute the sample size and duration`, `Randomize users and log assignment`, `Check for sample ratio mismatch`, `Analyze the primary metric and guardrails`, `Decide and document`],
          `Everything that could bias the result is decided before data arrives.`),
        BN(`New recommendation feature. Sort each metric.`, [`Primary`, `Guardrail`, `Diagnostic`],
          [[`Weekly watch time per user`, 0], [`Page load time`, 1], [`Unsubscribe rate`, 1], [`Clicks on the new carousel`, 2], [`Share of sessions that see the carousel`, 2]],
          `The primary metric reflects the goal, guardrails are things you must not break, and diagnostics explain why a result moved.`),
        BUG(`Find the data-leakage bug.`,
          [`X_scaled = StandardScaler().fit_transform(X)`, `X_tr, X_te, y_tr, y_te = train_test_split(X_scaled, y, test_size=0.2)`, `model = LogisticRegression().fit(X_tr, y_tr)`, `print(model.score(X_te, y_te))`],
          0,
          `The scaler learned its mean and spread from all rows, including the test set. Split first, fit the scaler on the training rows only, then transform both.`),
        Q(`Monty Hall: three doors, one car. You pick one, the host opens a goat door you did not pick, and offers a switch. What should you do?`,
          [`Switch: it wins with probability 2/3.`, `Stay: it wins with probability 2/3.`, `It does not matter: both doors are 1/2.`, `Switch only if the host opens the leftmost door.`],
          `Your first pick is right 1/3 of the time. The host's reveal moves the remaining 2/3 onto the other closed door.`),
        NUM(`You roll a fair die until a 6 appears. What is the expected number of rolls?`, 6,
          `Success probability is 1/6 per roll, so the expected waiting time is 1 / (1/6) = 6.`),
        NUM(`Salaries (in thousands): 40, 45, 50, 55, 360. What is the mean?`, 110,
          `Sum is 550, divided by 5 gives 110. The median is 50, which is why skewed data prefers the median.`),
        M(`Match each situation to the first thing you would do.`,
          [[`Fraud model with 0.5% positives`, `Use precision, recall and PR curves instead of accuracy`], [`Features include next month's balance`, `Remove the leaked feature and re-validate`], [`Training score far above validation score`, `Regularize, simplify or add data`], [`Metric dropped right after a logging change`, `Verify instrumentation before analyzing behavior`]],
          `Naming the specific tool for each failure mode is what separates practice from recital.`),
        RK(`Rank these model-evaluation setups from least to most trustworthy.`,
          [`Score measured on the training data`, `One random train/test split`, `K-fold cross-validation`, `Cross-validation plus a final untouched holdout`],
          `Each step reduces optimism: training score is inflated, one split is noisy, folds average the noise, and an untouched holdout guards against tuning to the folds.`),
        F(`Keep the most recent row per user.`, `df.sort_values("updated_at").___(["user_id"], keep="last")`, [`drop_duplicates`], [`unique`, `dedupe`, `drop_dups`],
          `drop_duplicates with keep="last" after sorting by time leaves each user's latest row.`),
        Q(`Last question: observational data shows users who enable notifications retain better. A PM wants to force notifications on for everyone. Your take?`,
          [`Correlation is not causation: engaged users probably enable them. Run a randomized test or an encouragement design before forcing it.`, `Ship it: the correlation between notifications and retention is strong enough that a randomized test would be a waste of time.`, `Ship it to everyone, then compare retention before and after the launch to see whether notifications helped.`, `Do nothing: causal questions cannot be answered with data, so the team should rely on the PM's judgment instead.`],
          `Selection bias explains the gap. Randomizing the prompt to some users separates the effect of notifications from who chooses them.`),
      ], "pr"),
    ],
  });

  /* ================= BUSINESS ANALYTICS ================= */
  GR.courseAddUnit("ba", {
    title: "Interview loop", sub: "SQL, metrics, experiments and product cases",
    lessons: [
      L("The questions they actually ask", "ba.sql", [
        T(`<p>Analyst loops tend to be a <b>SQL screen</b>, a <b>metrics or product case</b>, and an <b>experiment round</b>. The same few shapes keep coming back: window functions, joins that drop rows, dedup, "a metric fell 10%," and "how many X are there?" Learn the shapes, not the answers.</p>`),
        Q(`Return each customer's most recent order. Which approach is right?`,
          [`Number rows in a subquery with ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_date DESC), then keep rows numbered 1.`, `SELECT * FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id with MAX(order_date), which returns the full row for the latest date per customer.`, `ORDER BY order_date DESC LIMIT 1 inside a subquery joined back on customer_id, so each customer gets their newest order.`, `SELECT DISTINCT customer_id, order_date FROM orders ORDER BY order_date DESC, which keeps one row per customer.`],
          `ROW_NUMBER with a partition ranks within each customer. LIMIT 1 returns one row total, and selecting unaggregated columns with GROUP BY is invalid or arbitrary.`),
        Q(`List the customers who never placed an order.`,
          [`customers LEFT JOIN orders ON orders.customer_id = customers.id WHERE orders.id IS NULL`, `customers INNER JOIN orders ON orders.customer_id = customers.id WHERE orders.id IS NULL`, `customers JOIN orders ON ... GROUP BY customers.id HAVING COUNT(*) = 0`, `orders LEFT JOIN customers ON ... WHERE customers.id IS NULL`],
          `A LEFT JOIN keeps unmatched customers with NULLs on the order side. An inner join never produces unmatched rows, and the last option finds orphan orders instead.`),
        Q(`What is the difference between WHERE and HAVING?`,
          [`WHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups after aggregation.`, `WHERE is used for numeric columns and HAVING for text columns, and the engine picks based on the data type.`, `HAVING runs before WHERE, so it can drop rows early and speed up the query before any grouping happens.`, `They are interchangeable; HAVING is just older syntax that every modern database still accepts for the same filters.`],
          `Conditions on aggregates such as COUNT(*) greater than 3 can only go in HAVING, because the groups do not exist when WHERE runs.`),
        BN(`WHERE or HAVING? Sort each condition.`, [`WHERE (before grouping)`, `HAVING (after grouping)`],
          [[`status = 'paid'`, 0], [`COUNT(*) > 3`, 1], [`order_date >= '2025-01-01'`, 0], [`SUM(revenue) > 1000000`, 1], [`country IN ('US', 'CA')`, 0]],
          `Row-level conditions go in WHERE. Anything built from an aggregate belongs in HAVING.`),
        BUG(`Goal: every customer with their paid-order count, including customers with zero. Tap the buggy line.`,
          [`SELECT c.name, COUNT(o.id) AS orders`, `FROM customers c`, `LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id`, `WHERE o.status = 'paid'`, `GROUP BY c.name;`],
          3,
          `Filtering the right-hand table in WHERE throws away the NULL rows, turning the LEFT JOIN into an inner join. Move the condition into the ON clause: AND o.status = 'paid'.`),
        Q(`What does COUNT(email) count?`,
          [`Only the rows where email is not NULL`, `Every row in the group, including the rows where email is NULL or blank`, `Only the distinct emails, so a duplicated address is counted a single time`, `Only the rows where email is not empty and not NULL`],
          `COUNT(column) skips NULLs; COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(DISTINCT column) counts unique non-NULL values. An empty string is not NULL, so it is counted.`),
        Q(`"Daily active users fell 10% last week." Strongest first step?`,
          [`Check data and instrumentation, then size and timing, then segment by platform, region and user type, then the funnel.`, `Launch a re-engagement campaign to win back lapsed users right away, then measure whether the number recovers on its own.`, `Say it is probably seasonality, wait a week, and see whether the number comes back before digging into anything.`, `Ask the team which feature they think caused it, then investigate only that feature and ignore everything else.`],
          `Rule out a broken pipeline before explaining behavior, then narrow where and when it moved. Acting before diagnosing wastes effort.`),
        Q(`"How would you measure success for a new Save-for-later feature?"`,
          [`State the goal, choose one primary metric tied to it, add guardrails, and name a counter-metric that would reveal gaming.`, `Count total clicks on the Save button, since more clicks show that more people find the feature useful to them.`, `Use daily active users, since every feature affects it and it is the number leadership already tracks closely.`, `Survey five friends about whether they like the feature and report the average rating as the main result.`],
          `A good metric answer starts with the goal, not the data you happen to have. Clicks alone reward noise.`),
        Q(`"A VP wants a dashboard." What do you do first?`,
          [`Ask which decisions it will inform and how often, then choose a handful of metrics to match.`, `Build every available metric into it so nothing is missing and nobody has to come back with a follow-up request.`, `Pick a chart library and a layout first, since the look of the dashboard decides whether people will use it.`, `Copy last quarter's dashboard and refresh the numbers, because the team already knows how to read that layout.`],
          `Dashboards serve decisions. Starting from the decision keeps the page small and useful.`),
        Q(`"Estimate how many ride-share trips happen daily in a city of 2 million." Strongest approach?`,
          [`Split the population into groups, estimate the share who ride and how often, multiply, then sanity-check the total.`, `Search for the official ride-share number for the city and quote it, since a published figure beats any estimate.`, `Say it cannot be known without internal company data, and ask the interviewer to supply the figure instead.`, `Multiply 2 million by 10 trips each and move on, because a round number is easier to defend than a detailed one.`],
          `They want a structure with assumptions they can adjust, not a lucky number.`),
        NUM(`Ana has 2 orders, Ben has none, and Cy has 1. How many rows does customers LEFT JOIN orders return (one row per matched order, one row for each customer with none)?`, 4,
          `Ana gives 2 rows, Ben gives 1 row with NULLs, Cy gives 1 row. Total 4. An inner join would give 3.`),
      ], "learn"),

      L("Say it out loud", "ba.cases", [
        T(`<p>In a case round, the answer is a <b>shape</b>: clarify, break the metric apart, segment, quantify, recommend. Say each step aloud so the interviewer can follow, and finish with a clear recommendation, even a tentative one.</p>`),
        O(`"Our key metric dropped 10%." Order a strong spoken answer.`,
          [`Clarify: which metric, since when, how it is measured`, `Rule out data issues: tracking, pipeline, definitions`, `Decompose the metric into traffic, conversion and value`, `Segment by platform, region, user type and channel`, `Form hypotheses: launch, outage, seasonality, competitor`, `Recommend a fix and a way to confirm it`],
          `Clarify, verify, decompose, segment, hypothesize, act. Each step narrows the search before you propose anything.`),
        O(`"How many coffees are sold in a city each day?" Order the steps.`,
          [`Clarify scope: city, cups versus beans, time window`, `Break the problem into population x share who drink x cups each`, `Pick round numbers and say them aloud`, `Multiply the pieces`, `Sanity-check against another angle`],
          `Round numbers said aloud let the interviewer correct an assumption without derailing you.`),
        RK(`Rank these answers to "How do you know the A/B test won?" strongest first.`,
          [`Pre-set primary metric, enough sample, an interval that excludes zero and is practically meaningful, and clean guardrails`, `The p-value on the primary metric is below 0.05`, `The treatment line is higher on the dashboard`, `It won in one segment I found after slicing the data`],
          `Strong answers cover validity, size and side effects. A bare p-value ignores size and guardrails; a higher line and post-hoc segments are noise-prone.`),
        M(`Match each situation to the key point a strong answer must hit.`,
          [[`Signups spiked 40% on Tuesday`, `Check for bots, tracking changes or a campaign before celebrating`], [`Average order value rose but revenue fell`, `Orders fell: look at volume and mix, not just the average`], [`How would you size a market?`, `Top-down and bottom-up estimates that roughly agree`], [`Is DAU the right north star?`, `Depends on the goal; pair it with retention`], [`The test won on clicks and lost on revenue`, `Trust the metric tied to the business goal; clicks may be a vanity proxy`]],
          `Each situation has a trap. Naming the trap in the first sentence is the signal they want.`),
        M(`Match each term to a plain phrase.`,
          [[`Cohort`, `Users grouped by when they started`], [`Churn rate`, `Share of customers lost in a period`], [`ARPU`, `Revenue divided by active users`], [`Guardrail metric`, `Something you must not hurt while improving the main metric`], [`Simpson's paradox`, `A trend that reverses when groups are combined`]],
          `Plain definitions first; formulas only if asked.`),
        Q(`You get stuck on a live SQL problem. Best move?`,
          [`State the grain of the output, sketch the tables, write a skeleton query, then fill in pieces aloud.`, `Type silently until it works.`, `Ask to skip the question.`, `Paste a query that returns everything and filter later.`],
          `Naming the output grain (one row per what?) unlocks most SQL problems, and talking keeps the interviewer on your side.`),
        Q(`A stakeholder asks for a number you believe is flawed. What do you say?`,
          [`Give the number, flag the flaw in one sentence, and offer a better definition.`, `Refuse to give it.`, `Give it with no comment so you are not blamed.`, `Quietly change the definition and report that instead.`],
          `Be useful and honest together: deliver, caveat, and propose the fix.`),
      ], "learn"),

      L("Follow-ups they throw at you", "ba.ab", [
        T(`<p>A follow-up tests whether you understand the thing or just recited it. Expect "what if the baseline were different," "what if it is not significant," "how would you do it with ties?" Re-derive aloud, one changed number at a time.</p>`),
        Q(`A button test shows +1.2% relative conversion with p = 0.2. Follow-up: "So it is neutral?"`,
          [`No: it is inconclusive; the interval likely spans harm and benefit, so check whether the test was powered for this size.`, `Yes, it has no effect: a p-value of 0.2 shows the treatment and control groups are the same, so the change can be dropped.`, `Yes, ship it because the lift is positive, and a positive point estimate is better than nothing even when p is high.`, `Yes, but only after re-running the test repeatedly until p falls below 0.05, which confirms the true size of the lift.`],
          `A non-significant result is the absence of evidence, not evidence of absence. Power tells you what you could have detected.`),
        NUM(`Baseline conversion is 10% and you want to detect a 1 point absolute lift. Using n = 16 x p(1 - p) / delta squared, how many users per group?`, 14400,
          `16 x 0.1 x 0.9 = 1.44. Divide by 0.01 squared (0.0001) to get 14,400 per group.`),
        NUM(`Baseline 20%, detect a 2 point lift. Same rule of thumb: users per group?`, 6400,
          `16 x 0.2 x 0.8 = 2.56. Divided by 0.02 squared (0.0004) gives 6,400.`),
        NUM(`Revenue fell 10% while orders fell 4%. By what percent did average order value fall?`, 6.25,
          `Revenue = orders x AOV. 0.90 / 0.96 = 0.9375, so AOV is down 6.25%.`, undefined, 0.05, `%`),
        NUM(`Store A averages $10 over 100 orders; store B averages $20 over 300 orders. What is the combined average order value, in dollars?`, 17.5,
          `Weight by orders: (1,000 + 6,000) / 400 = 17.5. The simple average of 10 and 20 would wrongly give 15.`),
        NUM(`ARPU is $10 per month and monthly churn is 5%. Using lifetime = 1 / churn, what is the customer lifetime value, in dollars?`, 200,
          `Expected lifetime is 1 / 0.05 = 20 months. 20 x $10 = $200.`),
        NUM(`100,000 visitors; 20% add to cart, 50% of those start checkout, 80% of those pay. How many purchases?`, 8000,
          `100,000 x 0.2 = 20,000; x 0.5 = 10,000; x 0.8 = 8,000.`),
        Q(`Overall conversion went up, but every segment's conversion went down. Follow-up: "Explain."`,
          [`Mix shift (Simpson's paradox): the share of traffic moved toward a segment that converts better.`, `A calculation error; this cannot happen, because an overall rate must move in the same direction as its parts.`, `Random noise in the segments, which would disappear if we averaged over a few more weeks of data.`, `The segments are too small to analyze, so the overall number is the only one that can be trusted here.`],
          `The overall rate is a weighted average. If weight shifts to a high-converting segment, the total can rise while each part falls.`),
        Q(`Follow-up on your second-highest-salary-per-department query: "What if two people tie for highest?"`,
          [`Use DENSE_RANK so ties share a rank and the next distinct salary is rank 2.`, `Use ROW_NUMBER so every row gets a unique rank and exactly one person is treated as the highest earner.`, `Use RANK, which never skips numbers, so the second-highest salary is always ranked 2 even after a tie.`, `Ties do not matter in SQL, because the engine always breaks them the same way using the primary key.`],
          `With a tie for first, RANK would skip to 3 and ROW_NUMBER would break the tie arbitrarily. DENSE_RANK gives the next distinct value as 2.`),
        Q(`The lift was strong in week 1 and faded by week 3. Follow-up: "What do you think is happening?"`,
          [`A novelty effect: check new versus returning users and extend the test before trusting the early lift.`, `The feature got worse over time, so the early lift was real and the team should roll it back right now.`, `Statistical significance wears off after the first week, so the later weeks cannot be compared to it.`, `Week 1 is always the most reliable week, because it has the freshest users and the least noise.`],
          `Early excitement inflates the effect. Compare cohorts and run long enough for behavior to settle.`),
        BUG(`Goal: month-over-month revenue change. Tap the buggy line.`,
          [`SELECT month,`, `       revenue,`, `       revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (PARTITION BY month ORDER BY month) AS change`, `FROM monthly_revenue;`],
          2,
          `Partitioning by month puts each month alone in its own window, so LAG always returns NULL. Drop the PARTITION BY and order by month.`),
      ], "drill"),

      L("Mock loop", "ba.cases", [
        T(`<p>One full round, no hints: SQL, metrics, experiments and a product case. Answer aloud with a headline and a reason.</p>`),
        Q(`Which query gives a running total of daily revenue?`,
          [`SELECT day, SUM(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY day) AS running FROM daily_revenue`, `SELECT day, SUM(revenue) FROM daily_revenue GROUP BY day`, `SELECT day, SUM(revenue) OVER (PARTITION BY day) FROM daily_revenue`, `SELECT day, COUNT(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY day) FROM daily_revenue`],
          `SUM as a window with ORDER BY accumulates down the ordered rows. Partitioning by day would restart at each row.`),
        F(`Departments with more than 5 employees.`, `SELECT dept, ___(*) FROM emp GROUP BY dept HAVING ___(*) > 5;`, [`COUNT`, `COUNT`], [`SUM`, `AVG`],
          `The count of rows per department is an aggregate, so the filter belongs in HAVING.`),
        O(`Order the steps to investigate a sudden fall in checkout conversion.`,
          [`Confirm the drop is real: check tracking and definitions`, `Pin down the start time and compare it to releases`, `Break down by device, browser, country and payment method`, `Walk the funnel step by step`, `Propose a fix and a check to confirm it`],
          `A drop that starts at a release and sits in one browser points straight at the cause.`),
        BN(`Checkout redesign. Sort each metric.`, [`Primary`, `Guardrail`, `Diagnostic`],
          [[`Orders per visitor`, 0], [`Refund rate`, 1], [`Page load time`, 1], [`Clicks on the new promo box`, 2], [`Share of users who reach the payment step`, 2]],
          `The headline is the outcome you want, guardrails catch harm, and diagnostics explain the movement.`),
        BUG(`Goal: the number of unique visitors. Tap the buggy line.`,
          [`SELECT COUNT(user_id) AS unique_visitors`, `FROM page_views`, `WHERE view_date >= '2025-01-01';`],
          0,
          `COUNT(user_id) counts every non-NULL row, so repeat visits are counted again. Use COUNT(DISTINCT user_id).`),
        NUM(`Estimate: a city has 1,000,000 people, 40% buy coffee on a given day, and buyers average 1.5 cups. How many cups per day?`, 600000,
          `1,000,000 x 0.4 = 400,000 buyers; x 1.5 = 600,000 cups.`),
        NUM(`Of 1,000 users who signed up, 250 are still active in week 4. What is week-4 retention, in percent?`, 25,
          `250 / 1,000 = 25%.`, undefined, 0.01, `%`),
        RK(`Rank these dashboards from weakest to strongest.`,
          [`Forty metrics, no goals, no context`, `Ten metrics with no targets`, `Five metrics with targets and trends but no owner`, `Five metrics tied to named decisions, with targets, trends and owners`],
          `A dashboard improves as it ties to decisions, shows change against a target, and has someone accountable.`),
        M(`Match each experiment problem to its fix.`,
          [[`Users see both versions`, `Randomize at the user level, not the page view`], [`Test stopped early after a good day`, `Fix the sample size and duration in advance`], [`Many metrics checked, one hit`, `Choose one primary metric and correct for multiple tests`], [`Lift only in week 1`, `Extend the test and compare new versus returning users`]],
          `Each problem has a standard design fix. Naming it shows you have run tests, not just read about them.`),
        Q(`You cannot randomize a price change across users. What do you do?`,
          [`Roll out by region or time period and compare against matched control regions with difference-in-differences.`, `Change the price everywhere at once and compare revenue to last month, since that captures the full effect.`, `Skip measurement entirely; prices are a business call, and any analysis would be too noisy to be useful.`, `Survey customers about whether the new price feels fair, and treat the share who say yes as the effect.`],
          `Matched controls remove shared trends such as seasonality. A plain before/after mixes the effect with everything else that changed.`),
        Q(`Product case: time spent per session in a photo-sharing app rose 15% after a redesign, but weekly active users fell 3%. Your read?`,
          [`Ambiguous: check whether the redesign slowed tasks (more time, not more value) and where the user drop sits, then weigh retention.`, `A clear win: more time in each session means more engagement, and a small drop in users is normal after any redesign.`, `A clear loss: fewer weekly users always outweighs time per session, so the redesign should be rolled back immediately.`, `Noise: both changes are small enough that the team should ignore them and wait for next quarter's numbers instead.`],
          `More time can mean friction or enjoyment. Pair it with retention and task completion before calling a winner.`),
        Q(`Final: "What would you do in your first month?"`,
          [`Learn the key metrics and data sources, meet stakeholders about their decisions, and ship one small useful analysis.`, `Rebuild the whole data warehouse around cleaner definitions, so every future analysis starts from a clean base.`, `Wait for assignments from my manager, and avoid touching anything until I have been told exactly what to do.`, `Redefine all the metrics to match what I used at my last job, since those definitions are the ones I trust.`],
          `Learn first, deliver something small and visible, and avoid reshaping systems you do not yet understand.`),
      ], "pr"),
    ],
  });

  /* ================= FOUNDATIONS ================= */
  GR.courseAddUnit("fnd", {
    title: "Interview loop", sub: "Screens, plain English and how to prepare",
    lessons: [
      L("The questions they actually ask", "fnd.interview", [
        T(`<p>Early-career data screens are friendlier than the internet suggests: a few <b>Python and pandas basics</b>, one <b>explain it simply</b> question, and a chat about a project. Calm, clear and curious beats clever.</p>`),
        Q(`"Explain what a model is to a manager."`,
          [`A rule learned from past examples that predicts new cases, like estimating a home's price from similar homes that sold.`, `A parametric function f(x) = wx + b whose weights are fit by gradient descent to minimize mean squared error on training data.`, `A black box that uses AI to be accurate, so nobody needs to understand how it makes its decisions.`, `Math that predicts stuff.`],
          `Plain words and a concrete example. The second answer is correct but lands on a non-technical listener as noise.`),
        Q(`For a group where one person earns far more than the rest, which summary is better, and why?`,
          [`The median, because a few extreme values drag the mean upward.`, `The mean, because it uses every value and therefore reflects the whole group, including the high earner.`, `The mode, because it is the most common value and so best describes what a typical person earns.`, `Neither; skewed data cannot be summarized with one number, so you must always show the full list.`],
          `The median ignores how extreme the extremes are, so it describes a typical person better in skewed data.`),
        Q(`What does df.groupby("team")["score"].mean() return?`,
          [`The average score for each team, indexed by team`, `The average of the whole score column, repeated once for every team in the table`, `The first score listed for each team, taken in the order the rows appear in the file`, `A count of how many rows belong to each team, indexed by team`],
          `groupby splits rows by team, then mean averages the score column within each group.`),
        Q(`How is a NumPy array different from a Python list?`,
          [`An array holds one data type and does math on whole arrays at once; a list can mix types and needs loops.`, `A list is faster for math, because it stores each number as a separate Python object that can be added directly.`, `They are the same thing; the name differs because NumPy is just an alias for the built-in list type.`, `Arrays can only hold strings, so they are used for text, while lists are used for numeric work.`],
          `Vectorized array math runs in compiled code, which is why it is far faster than looping over a list.`),
        F(`Fill missing incomes with the column median.`, `df["income"] = df["income"].___(df["income"].median())`, [`fillna`], [`dropna`, `replace_na`, `isna`],
          `fillna replaces missing values. dropna removes rows instead; isna only flags them.`),
        BUG(`Goal: the three highest scores. Tap the buggy line.`,
          [`import pandas as pd`, `df = pd.read_csv('scores.csv')`, `top3 = df.sort_values('score').head(3)`, `print(top3)`],
          2,
          `sort_values sorts ascending by default, so this shows the three lowest scores. Add ascending=False, or use nlargest(3, 'score').`),
        Q(`They ask about the project on your resume. What will they probe?`,
          [`Why you chose that approach, what went wrong, and what you would change.`, `Only the final accuracy number, since that is the one fact on your resume the interviewer can compare.`, `The exact lines of code, which they expect you to recite from memory without looking at anything.`, `Which tutorial or course you followed, and whether you finished every exercise in it.`],
          `Projects are a window into your judgment. Prepare the tradeoffs and mistakes, not just the result.`),
        Q(`One week before the screen: best use of time?`,
          [`Practice small problems out loud, review your own projects, and prepare two short stories.`, `Read a new textbook cover to cover, so that you can cover every topic that might possibly come up.`, `Memorize algorithm proofs and derivations, because screens usually ask you to reproduce them in detail.`, `Avoid practicing at all so that you stay fresh, and rely on your experience to carry you through.`],
          `Speaking practice matters because screens are spoken. Focus beats breadth this close to the date.`),
      ], "learn"),

      L("Say it out loud", "fnd.workflow", [
        T(`<p>Explaining beats knowing. Practice saying each idea in one plain sentence, then one example. If your grandparent would not get it, shorten it again.</p>`),
        O(`Order a strong answer to "Walk me through a project."`,
          [`The problem and why it mattered`, `The data you used and how you cleaned it`, `The approach you chose and why`, `The result, with a number`, `Limits and what you would do next`],
          `Problem, data, method, result, limits: the order a listener can follow.`),
        M(`Match each term to a plain-English explanation.`,
          [[`Overfitting`, `Memorizing practice questions instead of learning the pattern`], [`Training data`, `The examples a model learns from`], [`Test set`, `Held-back examples used as a final exam`], [`Feature`, `An input column the model uses`], [`Accuracy`, `The share of predictions that were right`]],
          `Each analogy keeps one idea and drops the rest. Add numbers only when asked.`),
        RK(`Rank these answers to "Walk me through a project," strongest first.`,
          [`The problem, what you did and why, a result with a number, and what you would do next`, `The problem and the result, with few details on the method`, `Only the list of tools you used`, `"It was a class assignment."`],
          `Strong answers carry a story with a result and a next step. Tool lists and dismissals give the interviewer nothing to ask about.`),
        BN(`What should you say, and what should you skip?`, [`Say it`, `Skip it`],
          [[`"I would start by checking for missing values."`, 0], [`"I am not sure, but here is how I would find out."`, 0], [`"Trust me, the model is 99% accurate" (with no baseline)`, 1], [`Two minutes of silence`, 1], [`"It was a small dataset, so treat the result as a first look."`, 0]],
          `Honest limits and a plan build trust. Unsupported claims and long silences cost it.`),
        Q(`You do not know the answer. Best response?`,
          [`Say what you do know, say how you would find the rest, and ask a clarifying question.`, `Bluff with confidence, because interviewers rarely check details and hesitation is judged more harshly.`, `Say "I do not know" and stop talking, so the interviewer can move straight to the next question.`, `Change the subject to a topic you know well, so the conversation stays on your strongest material.`],
          `Interviewers expect gaps. They watch how you handle them.`),
      ], "drill"),

      L("Mock screen", "fnd.pandas", [
        T(`<p>Eight quick questions, the way a first screen goes. Answer each in a sentence before you tap.</p>`),
        NUM(`What does sum(nums) / len(nums) give for nums = [4, 8, 15, 16, 23, 42]?`, 18,
          `The sum is 108 and there are 6 values, so the mean is 18.`),
        NUM(`What is the sum of np.arange(2, 10, 2)?`, 20,
          `arange(2, 10, 2) gives 2, 4, 6, 8, which sum to 20. The stop value 10 is excluded.`),
        Q(`What does this print?`,
          [`4, because both names point to the same list`, `3, because b = a makes b an independent copy of the list`, `7, because the two lists are added together`, `An error, because b.append cannot be used on an alias`],
          `b = a does not copy; it gives the list a second name, so appending through b changes a too. Use a.copy() for an independent list.`,
          `a = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a\nb.append(4)\nprint(len(a))`),
        BUG(`Goal: the average score per team. Tap the buggy line.`,
          [`import pandas as pd`, `df = pd.read_csv('scores.csv')`, `avg = df.groupby('team')['score'].mean`, `print(avg)`],
          2,
          `mean without parentheses is the method itself, not its result. Call it with mean().`),
        O(`Order the steps of a small data task.`,
          [`Clarify the question`, `Load and inspect the data`, `Clean missing and odd values`, `Analyze or fit a model`, `Check the result on held-out data`, `Explain the findings in plain words`],
          `Clarify, look, clean, analyze, check, explain.`),
        M(`Match each pandas call to what it does.`,
          [[`df.head()`, `Shows the first few rows`], [`df.describe()`, `Summary statistics for numeric columns`], [`df.isna().sum()`, `Missing-value count per column`], [`df.merge(other, on="id")`, `Joins two tables on a shared key`], [`df["a"].value_counts()`, `Counts of each distinct value`]],
          `These five calls are enough to explore almost any unfamiliar table.`),
        Q(`A model is 95% accurate, but 95% of customers do not churn. What do you say?`,
          [`That is no better than always predicting "no churn"; check precision and recall on the churners.`, `Great, ship it: 95% accuracy is high enough that the model is clearly learning the churn pattern well.`, `95% is the industry standard, so the model is acceptable and no further checks are needed.`, `Accuracy is always the right metric, so the class balance does not change how we read the number.`],
          `With a 95% majority class, a model that never flags anyone also scores 95%. The rare class needs its own metrics.`),
      ], "pr"),
    ],
  });
})();
